## Abstract

SYNX-7 is a fictional small molecule reported to modulate stress-response signalling in the
fictional cell line SYNTH-CL1. To characterise its transcriptional footprint we profiled
SYNTH-CL1 cells exposed to SYNX-7 or to vehicle, three biological replicates each, by
single-end RNA sequencing. Reads were adapter- and quality-trimmed, quantified against the
<%= reference_release %> transcriptome by selective alignment, aggregated to genes, and tested for
differential expression with a negative-binomial generalised linear model. Of <%= gene_count %>
annotated genes, {{S2:n_de}} were differentially expressed at an adjusted p-value below 0.05
({{S2:n_up}} increased and {{S2:n_down}} decreased under SYNX-7). A label-permutation check on
the normalised fold changes is reported alongside the model-based test. The complete workflow,
environment specification and container recipe are provided, and every number in this report
can be regenerated from them.

## Introduction

Compounds that act on stress-response pathways often leave a broad but shallow
transcriptional signature, which makes the choice of quantification and testing procedure
consequential: small differences in trimming, in how multi-mapping reads between isoforms are
resolved, or in how dispersion is estimated can move genes across a significance threshold.
For that reason the analysis below is described step by step, with the version of every tool
and every parameter that departs from a tool's default, and the whole workflow is released as
an executable pipeline with a pinned environment.

SYNTH-CL1 is a fictional adherent line used here as a model system. SYNX-7 is a fictional
compound. The reference, <%= reference_release %>, is a fictional transcriptome of
<%= transcript_count %> transcripts from <%= gene_count %> genes; many genes carry more than one
isoform, and isoforms of the same gene share most of their sequence, so transcript-level
quantification has to apportion reads between them.

The aim of the study is descriptive: to list genes whose expression changes under SYNX-7 and
to estimate the size of those changes. We make no claim about mechanism.

## Methods

### Experimental design

SYNTH-CL1 cells were exposed either to vehicle (control) or to SYNX-7 (treated), with three
biological replicates per condition, giving six samples: SYNTH-S01, SYNTH-S02 and SYNTH-S03
(control) and SYNTH-S04, SYNTH-S05 and SYNTH-S06 (treated). The sample sheet is
`config/samples.tsv` in the code repository.

### Sequencing data

Each sample was sequenced single-end with 100 bp reads. The read files are
`data/synthetic_reads/synthetic_SYNTH-S01.fastq.gz` to `synthetic_SYNTH-S06.fastq.gz` in the
code repository. Base qualities are encoded as Phred+33. The reads are synthetic; how they can be
regenerated is stated under Data and code availability.

### Reference

Reads were quantified against the <%= reference_release %> transcriptome
(`data/synthetic_reference/<%= reference_stem %>.transcripts.fa`, <%= transcript_count %>
transcripts). Transcripts were mapped to genes with the transcript-to-gene table distributed
with the same release (`data/synthetic_reference/<%= reference_stem %>.tx2gene.tsv`,
<%= gene_count %> genes). Transcript identifiers carry a version suffix (for example
`SYNT0001.1`), which was kept throughout.

### Read trimming

Adapters and low-quality bases were removed with fastp <%= tool('fastp') %>, one thread per sample,
with the adapter sequence given explicitly (`--adapter_sequence <%= adapter %>`), a
per-base quality threshold of Phred 20 (`--qualified_quality_phred 20`) and a minimum read
length after trimming of 30 bases (`--length_required 30`). All other options were left at
their defaults. fastp's per-sample JSON reports were retained for quality control.

### Transcript quantification

A salmon <%= tool('salmon') %> index was built from the <%= reference_release %> transcript
sequences with a k-mer length of 31 (`salmon index --kmerLen 31`), without decoy sequences.
Trimmed reads were quantified per sample with `salmon quant` in selective-alignment mode
(`--validateMappings`), with automatic library-type detection (`--libType A`), single-end
input (`--unmatedReads`) and one thread (`--threads 1`). No bias correction and no bootstrap
replicates were requested; all other options were left at their defaults.

### Gene-level aggregation

Transcript-level estimates were summarised to genes with tximport <%= tool('bioconductor-tximport') %>
in R <%= tool('r-base') %> (`type = "salmon"`, the <%= reference_release %> transcript-to-gene table,
other arguments at their defaults), producing estimated counts, abundances and average
transcript lengths per gene and sample.

### Differential expression

Differential expression was tested with DESeq2 <%= tool('bioconductor-deseq2') %>. The count
matrix and average transcript lengths from tximport were imported with
`DESeqDataSetFromTximport`, with the design `~ condition` and control as the reference level.
Size factors, dispersions and coefficients were estimated with `DESeq` at default settings. The
treated-versus-control contrast was extracted with `results(..., alpha = 0.05)`, which applies
independent filtering at that level; p-values were adjusted by the Benjamini-Hochberg
procedure. A gene was called differentially expressed when its adjusted p-value was below 0.05.
No fold-change shrinkage was applied; the reported log2 fold changes are the maximum-likelihood
estimates.

### Permutation check

As a check that does not depend on the negative-binomial model, the treatment labels of the six
samples were permuted <%= cfg[:permutation_draws] %> times at random, and for every gene the
difference in mean log2(normalised count + 1) between the two groups was recomputed under each
permutation. The permutation p-value is (1 + the number of permutations whose absolute
difference is at least the observed one) / (<%= cfg[:permutation_draws] %> + 1). The random
number generator was seeded with <%= seed %> (R's default generator) so that the permutation
p-values are reproducible.

### Quality control

Trimming and quantification reports were collated with MultiQC <%= tool('multiqc') %>.

### Workflow and environment

All steps were run as a Snakemake <%= tool('snakemake-minimal') %> workflow (`Snakefile` in the code
repository) with one core. The software environment is specified in `envs/rnaseq.yml` with
exact versions and build strings, and locked for linux-64 with conda-lock
(`envs/rnaseq.lock`, one package URL and checksum per line). The results reported here were
produced inside the Docker image built from the repository's `Dockerfile`, whose base image is
referenced by digest and whose packages are installed from the lock file. Parameters that are
not command-line options (paths, adapter, thresholds, number of permutations and the permutation
seed) are recorded in `config/config.yaml`.

